21:02:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I completely understand why you feel that way. It is profoundly unsettling to realize that something you’ve believed for a long time—especially something that feels like it’s been confirmed by the people around you and your own observations—might not be accurate. It can absolutely make you question your own judgment and feel like the ground is shifting under your feet. That feeling of discomfort is very real and very common. It’s what psychologists sometimes call "cognitive dissonance"—the mental discomfort that happens when we encounter new information that contradicts our existing beliefs. Here are a few thoughts on why this happens and why it doesn’t mean you can’t trust your judgment at all: ### 1. We All Rely on Mental Shortcuts Every single person relies on mental shortcuts (heuristics) to make sense of the world. We can’t analyze every single piece of information we encounter from scratch, so we rely on patterns, what our community tells us, and our past experiences. Most of the time, these shortcuts work well! They help us navigate daily life safely and efficiently. But when it comes to complex topics—like understanding entire cultures or populations we haven't deeply studied—these shortcuts often fail us. They lead us to rely on stereotypes. Realizing a shortcut failed doesn't mean your brain is broken; it just means you've found a situation where you need to use a different tool. ### 2. "Life Experience" is Limited Our "life experience" is incredibly valuable, but it is also inherently limited. * **The Bubble Effect:** We tend to surround ourselves with people who think like us and share our background. If everyone in your circle shares a certain belief, it feels like universal truth, even if it’s just the consensus of your specific group. * **Confirmation Bias:** As I mentioned before, our brains are wired to notice things that confirm what we already believe and ignore things that don't. If you believe a place is dangerous, you will remember the one aggressive person and forget the ten polite ones. Recognizing these limitations doesn't mean your experiences aren't real; it just means they might not represent the whole picture. ### 3. Growth Requires Discomfort The fact that you are willing to sit with this unsettling feeling and acknowledge it is actually a sign of intellectual honesty. Many people, when faced with information that challenges their worldview, simply double down and get angry. Questioning your assumptions is how learning happens. It’s how we grow. It doesn't mean you have to throw out everything you know. It means you are developing a more nuanced, sophisticated way of looking at the world. ### How to Move Forward You don't need to stop trusting your judgment; you just need to refine it. When you encounter a broad claim or a strong feeling about a group of people, you can start asking yourself: * "Where did I learn this?" * "Is this based on a few anecdotes or broad evidence?" * "Does this belief allow for the complexity of human beings?" It is okay to feel unsettled. It means you are engaging with new ideas. You don't have to change your mind overnight, but being open to the possibility that the world is more complex than a simple stereotype is a significant step.
